            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article discusses several issues related to therapies that are considered &quot;complementary&quot; or &quot;alternative&quot; to conventional medicine. A definition of &quot;complementary and alternative medicine&quot; (CAM) is considered in the context of the evolving health care field of complementary medicine. A rationale for pain physicians and clinicians to understand these treatments of chronic pain is presented. The challenges of an evidence-based approach to incorporating CAM therapies are explored. Finally, a brief survey of the evidence that supports several widely available and commonly used complementary therapies for chronic pain is provided.</AbstractText>
